anti-pattern OvercrowdedPan {
  id: "@recipes/anti-pattern-overcrowded-pan"
  version: "1.0.0"

  label: "Overcrowding the Pan"
  domain: cooking

  description: "Adding too much food to a pan at once — so that pieces touch and steam accumulates — causing the food to steam instead of sear. The Maillard reaction requires a dry, hot surface; a crowded pan generates moisture that keeps the surface temperature below 100°C."

  why-bad: "Steam is produced when evaporating moisture from wet food surfaces cannot escape fast enough. This drops the pan temperature and prevents the surface from reaching the 140°C+ required for Maillard browning. The result: grey, soft, and watery food instead of the desired browned, caramelized exterior. Flavor and texture both suffer."

  instead-do: "Cook in batches sized so that pieces are in a single layer with at least 1 cm between them. A 28 cm skillet fits about 200 g of mushrooms or 2 medium chicken thighs. Rest cooked batches in a warm oven (80°C) while the next batch cooks. The total cook time is longer but the result is dramatically better."

  notes: [
    "This applies equally to sautéed vegetables, seared meat, and stir-fries.",
    "Patting food dry before adding to the pan reduces the moisture load — extends the window before crowding becomes a problem.",
    "Cast iron and carbon steel retain heat better than thin stainless under load, reducing the severity of temperature drop when food is added.",
  ]

  related: [
    @recipes/fact-maillard-reaction-temperature,
    @recipes/anti-pattern-cold-pan-searing,
    @recipes/pattern-stir-fry-mise,
  ]

  see-also: [
    @recipes/anti-pattern-cold-pan-searing,
  ]
}
